lesson: 141
title: Numbers to 1000: adding and taking away in stories
level: primary-3
stars: 0
explain: A number to 1000 has hundreds, tens and ones: 476 is 4 hundreds, 7 tens and 6 ones.
explain: To add or take away big numbers, write them one under the other and work column by column, ones first: carry ten ones as one ten, or break one ten into ten ones.
explain: A library had 476 books, bought 158 and gave away 290: 476 + 158 = 634, and 634 - 290 = 344 books.
vars: a=name n=200..650 m=100..350 k=50..400
need: k < n
need: n % 10 != 0 && m % 10 != 0
answer: n + m - k
exercise: A library had {n} books. It bought {m} new books and gave {k} old books to a school. How many books does the library have now?
exercise: A school had {n.words} pupils. In September {m} new pupils came and {k} pupils left. How many pupils does the school have now?
exercise: A farm had {n} hens. The farmer bought {m} more hens and sold {k} hens. How many hens are on the farm now?
exercise: {a} had {n} stamps in {a.his} album. {a.He} got {m} more stamps and then gave {k} stamps to a friend. How many stamps are in the album now?
exercise: There were {n} people on a train. At the station {m} people got on and {k} people got off. How many people are on the train now?
exercise: A shop had {n} kg of potatoes. It got {m} kg more in the morning and sold {k} kg during the day. How many kilograms of potatoes are left?
test: {k} of the seats in a theatre were taken away for repairs, and {m} new seats were put in. Before that the theatre had {n} seats. How many seats does it have now?
test: How many sheep are in the flock now, if it had {n.words} sheep, {m} lambs were born in spring, and {k} sheep were sold?
test: A baker made {n} rolls in the morning and {m} rolls in the afternoon. By the evening {k} rolls had been sold. How many rolls remain?
test: {a} saved {n} dollars, then earned {m} dollars more. A new bike cost {a.him} {k} dollars. How much money does {a} have after buying the bike?
exercise: How many books does a library have now, if it had {n} books, bought {m} new ones and gave away {k} old ones?
exercise: {n} books, {m} more came in, {k} went out. How many books are there now?
exercise: A school in Ohio had {n} pupils. Then {m} new pupils joined and {k} pupils moved away. How many pupils does it have now?
exercise: A zoo in London had {n.words} visitors in the morning. By noon {m.words} more came and {k.words} went home. How many visitors are still at the zoo?
exercise: A farm in Alberta had {n} sheep. {m} lambs were born, and the farmer sold {k} sheep. How many sheep does the farm have now?
exercise: There were {n} cars in a car park in Sydney. {m} cars drove in and {k} cars drove out. How many cars remain in the car park?
exercise: {a} had {n} points in a game. {a.He} won {m} points and lost {k} points. How many points does {a.he} still have?
exercise: A bookshop in Dublin had {n} comics. It got {m} more and sold {k}. How many comics are left?
test: {m} new books arrived at a library in Vermont that had {n}, and then {k} books were handed out to a charity. What is the library's total now?
test: A ferry in Auckland left port with {n} passengers. At the island {m} people boarded and {k} went ashore. How many passengers are on board now?
test: A charity in Glasgow had {n} blankets. People donated {m} more, and the charity handed out {k}. How many blankets does it still have?
test: How many tickets are left, if a stadium in Toronto had {n} tickets, printed {m} more and sold {k}?
test: {a} counted {n} ants in the morning, {m} more crawled in, and then {k} crawled away. How many ants remain?
test: After getting {m} dollars and spending {k} dollars, how many dollars does {a} have, if {a.he} started with {n.words} dollars?
exercise: library had {n} books, got {m} new, gave {k} away. how many now
exercise: farm had {n+10} hens, no sorry {n}. bought {m} more, sold {k}. how many hens now
exercise: school had {n}, {m} came, {k} left. my mate did {n} + {m} + {k} = {n+m+k}. nah, how many pupils now
